{"ts": 1789777332.8831081, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "The sign checks are all coherent: flipping any component collapses the Sharpe, confirming 17, 29, and 13 each carry real directional signal in the same combination. I'll go with the triple.\n\nNow the honest part \u2014 predicting OOS Sharpe. I should **not** predict 1.014.\n\n**Sources of upward bias in that number:**\n- I evaluated ~60 specifications and am reporting the maximum. The max of many noisy estimates overshoots the truth.\n- The selection was concentrated: I chose 17 and 29 as the top 2 of 40 single features, then chose 13 as the best of ~20 third features. Each stage adds selection bias.\n- The third-feature sweep gives me a direct read on the bias: random third features clustered at ~0.87, and the best of ~20 draws reached 1.014. That ~0.14 gap is largely selection noise, not signal.\n\n**What's likely real:** features 17 and 29 were genuine outliers (0.716, 0.613) against a field where everything else was \u22640.42 \u2014 that separation is too large to be pure luck across only 40 features. Their combination at 0.943, well above either alone, indicates real diversification. So there is a solid core here.\n\n**My estimate:** I shrink the pair's 0.943 toward ~0.80 for selection over 40 features, and treat feature 13's marginal contribution as mostly illusory, worth perhaps +0.03 rather than +0.07. That lands me near **0.82**.\n\nFor uncertainty: with n=5000 the pure sampling SE is ~0.14, but OOS error is dominated by regime change and by the chance my selection was luckier than I've credited. I'll use **sd = 0.30**, which puts meaningful mass below 0.5 \u2014 appropriate given that the in-sample figure sits right at the search-adjusted bar rather than comfortably above it."}
